WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
 So the service-oriented approach is a way we approach solving a complex application

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.760
 problem where all the data really isn't present in one computer system, it's somehow spread

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:17.760
 out over the internet, connected via the internet or internal network, and so the idea is that

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:24.840
 some applications just can't contain everything. The perfect example is a travel website that

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:30.720
 can book you a flight, book you a car, buy tickets, book you a hotel, and do all these

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.800
 things. Well, that travel website is neither a hotel nor a rental car company nor an airline,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.160
 but what it really does is it talks to all these services somewhere else on the web on

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.840
 your behalf, and it makes reservations for you, and so you have this convenient user

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.640
 interface that says, oh, here's your whole vacation, I'm going to figure all this stuff

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:55.360
 out, now you say go, and it goes book, book, book, book, and books on all these other systems.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.680
 Now it requires a lot of infrastructure, a lot of coordination, and a lot of effort

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.240
 to make sure that your application can talk, and these other services that are out there

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:10.880
 in the internet have good contracts, and you know exactly how to send data to them and

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.280
 get data back from them. And so initially, when you're building a service-oriented architecture,

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.380
 often you have one application, and it's all internal, often it's all one language, and

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.240
 then maybe you'll say, oh, wait a sec, we want to take part of what we do and put it in

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.880
 a second system, and then sort of come up with a set of rules between the systems, and

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.120
 then more and more and more. And this is a video that talks a little bit about the process

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:41.280
 and complexity about taking these interfaces between applications and applying standards

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.400
 to those interfaces.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:02:01.280
 [MUSIC]

